selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166304 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186166309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166304 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "648b28e8-61ba-5183-92e3-64a3e48338c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166305 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "845c09e6-5d17-5bb5-adbc-3721e754fcd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166306 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b42d62fd-5de8-57e7-89e2-4a835c65eec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166307 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166307 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166307 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3bac43d-fbd2-555d-98fa-4f3cf841dda7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166308 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ece24b1c-2340-5941-9ac1-1dce184ab664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166309 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166309 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166309 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52958bf7-76a5-59bb-8124-ef40c1e1ee10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166680 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166680 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair die has $9$ sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $9.$ What is the probability of getting a number greater than or equal to $6$ on the next throw of the die?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166680 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dad3d0a2-26e0-52c4-9e5a-abe6664cc02c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166680 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number the die lands on. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X \\geq 6).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/04/04a4d9a4cad75503f6dead488d088b95d9e44cde6ca44e73a33cb4e5a242111d.svg)\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim U \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ and the set $S = \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}$ has $|S| = 9-1 + 1 = 9$ items. So, we have\n\n![](images/c3/c34845ee8e867275c17efed55359d7fea4414e2688c25356b28df73c48c974f9.svg)\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X \\geq 6$ are $6, 7, 8, 9.$ Since there are $4$ elements in total, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 6) = 4 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166687 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166687 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time, in seconds, needed to download a video.\n\nII. The number obtained when rolling a fair $4$-sided die whose sides are labeled with the numbers $5, 6, 7, 8.$\n\nIII. The number of rainy days in a randomly selected summer month in New York City." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166687 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de4ae41e-c981-5b5e-b52c-c8e7caf4c272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166687 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each given random variable.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because an amount of time is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of sides, and since the die is fair, each integer between $5$ and $8$ has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because each possible number of rainy days is not equally likely. For example, it's more likely that there will be $5$ rainy days than $31$ rainy days.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166838 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166841 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling With Discrete Uniform Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167033 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "631d41d0-caaf-5a9a-8598-3ad89711698e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167513 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking an integer between $1$ and $10$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $1$ and $10$ at random.\n\nIII. The color of the ball obtained when a ball from a bag containing $3$ blue and $4$ red balls is chosen at random." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "200aef1f-7404-50e8-879d-3c216fa12872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167513 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of integers between $1$ and $10,$ and since the selection is made at random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $1$ and $10,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of selecting a red ball.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167514 #edb/ref 1071 "In a videogame, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $100$ and $155,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability to occur, what is the probability of having less than $130$ obstacles?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29f6e184-5ce2-54d0-991f-fb9cc535577d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167514 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{100,101,…,155\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<130).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{100,101,…,155\\},$ and the set $S=\\{100,101,…,155\\}$ has $|S|=155-100+1=56$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{56}, & x=100,101,…,155 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<130$ are $100,101,…,129.$ There are $30$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<130)=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{56}=\\frac{15}{28}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167516 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly.\n\nII. The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel.\n\nIII. The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3f94f8a-9ac9-561e-91b8-f484694ecc10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167516 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50%$ than $100%.$\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167679 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167679 #edb/ref 1061 32013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79d64508-f3b9-51ba-b040-3e3b55ece653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167680 #edb/ref 1061 32014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cf23694-2167-55c4-9d0e-c4724313b90c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167684 #edb/ref 1061 32093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "832deb31-bd88-5bbe-acc1-91719627f9fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167769 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167769 #edb/ref 1061 39012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167769 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1abe1dd3-86a6-56dd-9bbf-f7a1402c711c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167769 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168077 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a discrete uniform distribution is a probability distribution that models the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/d0/d09ac993190a8f4a98e2703e8a0caa2decfbc193486bdf0352d4210e4738eb66.svg)\nwhere $|S|$ is the size (cardinality) of $S$.\n\nDiscrete uniform distributions occur regularly in games of chance. \n\nFor example, suppose that in a particular lottery, players must select a number from the following set: \n\n$$\n\\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}\n$$\n\nLet's assume that a player randomly selects a number from this set such that each number has an equal probability of being selected. Then, if $X$ represents the number the player chooses, we can model this situation using a discrete uniform distribution, where\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFurther, since $S = \\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}$ has $|S| = 50$ items, the probability mass function for the random variable $X$ is\n\n![](images/4a/4a4a14ac1547b6b995d77c579c46ce63048a9285166059708ce39c8739fdbb7d.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168077 #edb/ref 1065 5101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168077 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c576c848-4a58-54d4-a011-7e6c489c8225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168077 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168134 #edb/ref 1064 "To apply a discrete uniform probability model to a situation in context, there are two things we need to check for:\n\n- The support $S$ of our random variable should be *finite*, and\n\n- each $x\\in S$ must be *equally likely*.\n\nThere are some common pitfalls that we need to be aware of!\n\n- First, we cannot use a discrete uniform distribution to model continuous phenomena.\n\nFor example, weight, time, and distance are all quantities that can take *any* real number within a given interval. Such quantities should be modeled as continuous, so a discrete uniform distribution is usually inappropriate.\n\n- Second, we must be wary of discrete phenomena requiring infinitely large support.\n\nFor example, suppose $X$ represents the number of road accidents at a particular junction in a given month. Now, while $X$ is (hopefully) a very small number, it could theoretically be *any* positive integer for modeling purposes! For this reason, a variable with infinite support is more appropriate. Moreover, $X$ is unlikely to be uniformly distributed since having $X=2$ accidents per month is more likely than $X=1\\,000\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168134 #edb/ref 1065 6457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168134 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aabc2db5-2a28-567d-94ed-1b215e353ccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168134 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining When a Discrete Uniform Distribution is Appropriate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424332 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424332 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424332 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e218fff-12f9-5fa5-922d-87fcb0d3466e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424333 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #ed