17] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424372 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d8df995d-74a1-505c-a3a5-e60115444fb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424373 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e77545cc-1b3a-5e85-b752-ea1b743f1c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424374 #edb/ref 1071 "A child is playing with a tablet. The apps on the tablet are labeled with numbers $4$ through $21.$ If the child chooses an app at random, what is the probability that the app number they pick is more than $16?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f759cee-8f8e-57dc-b752-40d429fccaa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424374 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the app picked. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>16).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,21\\}$ has $|S|=21-4+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=4,5,…,21 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>16$ are $17,18,19,20,21.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>16)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{5}{18}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424375 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The temperature of a town on a certain day.\n\nII. The number of letters of a word randomly selected from an English dictionary.\n\nIII. The number of typos in a randomly selected book." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "239e34ce-3c73-5bae-b143-3d41bff8bf51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424375 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because a temperature is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely (there are more short words than very long words, for example).\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of typos can be any positive integer. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424376 #edb/ref 1071 "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $80$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store has neither the first $3$ issues nor the final issue in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the probability that he chooses either issue $20$ or issue $23?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "574dc243-2e55-5ce7-9e9d-a12e501cd5c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424376 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,79\\}$ has $|S|=79-4+1=76$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{76}, & x=4,5,…,79 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy the given condition are $20,23.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{76}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424377 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking an integer between $0$ and $10$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $5$ and $9$ at random.\n\nIII. The number of clients arriving at a shopping mall in a day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78d51529-5603-5294-836c-698a99289cb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424377 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $0$ and $10,$ and since the selection is random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $5$ and $9,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of clients arriving at a shopping mall can be any positive integer, and each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424378 #edb/ref 1071 "When rolling a fair six-sided die, what is the probability of getting a prime number?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a490336d-2a2e-5374-a15f-1401e77380a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424378 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on which the die lands. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,6\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1,2,…,6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}$ are $2,3,5.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\})=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424379 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424379 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424379 #edb/ref 1071 "The only available spaces in a multi-story parking lot are on the second floor, numbered from $215$ to $268.$\nIf Emma parks in one of the spaces at random, what is the probability that she parks in a space which is numbered between $234$ and $245$ inclusive?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e66fdc5b-c55e-53fe-9d08-47c56ef73570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424379 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the parking space Emma picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(234\\le X\\le 245).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and the set $S=\\{215,216,…,268\\}$ has $|S|=268-215+1=54$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{54}, & x=215,216,…,268 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $234\\le X\\le 245$ are $234,235,…,245.$ There are $245-234+1=12$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(234\\le X\\le 245)=12\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424380 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when throwing a standard die that is biased toward landing on $1.$\n\nIII. The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc3edf0b-9469-5401-acc3-594a44dac66e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424380 #edb/ref 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of cards, and since the selection is made at random, each card has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the die is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $1.$\n\n- Random variable III can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
